ConfigMaps and Secrets — learn these end to end
These are another priority because you haven't worked with them deeply yet.
- ConfigMap: Stores non-sensitive configuration, such as application mode, log level, or a service URL.
- Secret: Stores sensitive configuration, such as passwords, tokens, and credentials. Kubernetes Secrets are not automatically encrypted 
           just because they are Secret objects; configure encryption at rest and suitable access controls.

Step 1: Create configuration

apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: app-config
data:
  APP_ENV: "production"
  LOG_LEVEL: "info"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: app-secret
type: Opaque
stringData:
  DB_USERNAME: "appuser"
  DB_PASSWORD: "replace-with-a-secure-value"

stringData lets you supply readable strings; Kubernetes converts them into the Secret's encoded data representation. Do not commit real passwords to Git.

Step 2: Inject values into a Pod

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: config-demo
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app
      image: busybox:1.36
      command: ["sh", "-c", "env; sleep 3600"]
      env:
        - name: APP_ENV
          valueFrom:
            configMapKeyRef:
              name: app-config
              key: APP_ENV
        - name: DB_USERNAME
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: app-secret
              key: DB_USERNAME
        - name: DB_PASSWORD
          valueFrom:
            secretKeyRef:
              name: app-secret
              key: DB_PASSWORD


Step 3: Apply and verify

kubectl apply -f config.yaml
kubectl apply -f pod.yaml

kubectl get configmap app-config
kubectl describe configmap app-config
kubectl get secret app-secret
kubectl describe pod config-demo
kubectl logs config-demo
